<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mogao Caves</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Caves of the Thousand Buddhas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mogao Caves are a complex of nearly 500 Buddhist cave temples cut into a cliff south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, from the 4th to the 14th century. They hold some of the finest Buddhist murals and sculptures in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. In 1900 a sealed library cave was found there containing tens of thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, the Dunhuang manuscripts, and the site became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1987.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mogao-caves.jpg" alt="Mogao Caves" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: xiquinhosilva · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mogao_Caves_(54376969262).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> was founded as a frontier garrison by the Han emperor Wudi in 111 BCE to guard against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, and became a gateway to the West on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>. The Mogao Caves began, according to a book from the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-zetian">Empress Wu Zetian</a>, in 366 CE, when the monk Le Zun had a vision of a thousand Buddhas bathed in golden light at the site and decided to build a cave there. A second monk, Faliang, joined him, and a community of monks formed by the time of the Northern Liang. The caves first served hermit monks for meditation. Caves continued to be cut until the 14th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The cliff stretches nearly two kilometres. At its height under the Tang, more than a thousand caves existed, and 735 survive. The 487 in the southern section, which are decorated, were places of pilgrimage and worship, while 248 in the north were living quarters, meditation chambers and burial sites for monks. The murals cover about 46,000 square metres, from the 5th to the 14th century, and their quality peaked in the Tang period. About 2,400 clay sculptures survive. The largest, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maitreya">Maitreya</a> in cave 96, 35.5 metres high, was built in 695 under the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-zetian">Empress Wu Zetian</a>.</p><p>Early murals show strong Indian and Central Asian influence, and a distinct <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> style emerged under the Northern Wei. Local rich clans built several caves as family caves, which show their devotion and social standing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>On 25 June 1900 Wang Yuanlu, a Taoist abbot who had made himself guardian of some of the temples, found a walled-up small cave, Cave 17, packed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>. It had been sealed in the 11th century and may originally have been a memorial cave for the monk Hongbian, who died in 862. It held up to 50,000 manuscripts dating from the 5th to the early 11th century, mostly Chinese and Buddhist but also in Tibetan, Uighur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, Sogdian and Khotanese. They include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diamond-sutra">Diamond Sutra</a> of 868, the earliest dated printed book. The manuscripts were later scattered, with the largest collections now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mogao Caves” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 1920s and 1930s Marc Aurel Stein and others found thousands of scrolls in the <b>Mogao Caves</b> near Dunhuang, including more than 50 partial and complete manuscripts and a copy of the lost Xiang'er commentary.</blockquote><a href="/work/tao-te-ching">Tao Te Ching · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Its rulers financed cave temples at <b>Mogao</b>, and its monasteries produced the Dunhuang manuscripts in Chinese, Tibetan, Khotanese, Sogdian and Uyghur.</blockquote><a href="/state/guiyi-circuit">Guiyi Circuit</a></li></ul></section>
